<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
  <%# Declared above the include, not below it: head.ejs writes the Twitter
     card from these two and a const declared after it is not in scope there.
     See the twitter:title comment in views/head.ejs. %>
  <% const title = 'Add New Game - OldSchoolGames' %>
  <% const description = 'Add a new classic MS-DOS game to the OldSchoolGames collection. Admin interface for managing retro games database.' %>
  <%- include('../head', { title, description }) %>
  <%#
     No Open Graph here, which is what the other three admin forms already do
     and what this one is being brought into line with.

     These tags exist to describe a page to whatever is about to render a
     preview card for it. Nothing ever will: the route is behind isAuth and
     isAdmin, so a scraper following a pasted link is handed the login page,
     and the only thing that links here is the button views/index.ejs draws
     for a signed-in admin. A card nobody can produce is bytes on every
     render of the form for no reader.

     The og:url the block carried is the clearest evidence that nothing read
     it. On the edit form beside this one it named "/games/edit" — an address
     this router does not answer and never has, the real one being
     /games/:id/edit. It sat there through every review because no consumer
     exists to have noticed.

     tests/views/meta-tags.test.ts is where this is held: declaring og:title
     is what obliges a page to carry an og:image with it, so the check is
     conditional on that tag, and the four admin forms are the reason it is.
  %>
  <title><%= title %></title>
  <meta name="description" content="<%= description %>" />
</head>

<body class="layout">
  <%- include('../navbar') %>
  <%- include('../left-sidebar') %>
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>New Game</h1>
      <%- include('game-form') %>
    </div>
  </main>
  <%- include('../right-sidebar') %>
  <%- include('../footer') %>
</body>

</html>
